                                 | (4.1, 4.2 step 5a, 5.x records).  |\\\\n| VM-local counters collide on one  | Agreed. Link id is `[origin       |\\\\n| response stream                   | counter]`; origin minted per task |\\\\n|                                   | by the scheduler owning the pair; |\\\\n|                                   | multiplexed schedulers need       |\\\\n|                                   | distinct prefixes (7.2 step 3).   |\\\\n| Exported closures carry child     | Agreed. `linked` is load, lift to |\\\\n| code coordinates                  | UCF closure markers relative to   |\\\\n|                                   | the image identity, lower against |\\\\n|                                   | the parent's alias column or      |\\\\n|                                   | offset (7.3). Divergence: export  |\\\\n|                                   | relocation chosen over execution  |\\\\n|                                   | in the owning child, which would  |\\\\n|                                   | be a cross-VM function call.      |\\\\n| `:fallback :verifying` could      | Agreed. `:derivation` policy is   |\\\\n| accept an unverified claim        | `:verifying` or `:trusted`;       |\\\\n|                                   | verifying fetches the tree and    |\\\\n|                                   | re-lowers under the exact         |\\\\n|                                   | per-format profile or refuses     |\\\\n|                                   | `:unverified-derivation`; it      |\\\\n|                                   | never downgrades (8.1, 6.2).      |\\\\n| Bare `:asserted-by` honored       | Agreed. Proof required: a         |\\\\n|                                   | signature over canonical bytes    |\\\\n|                                   | verified by a composition-        |\\\\n|                                   | supplied function, or an attested |\\\\n|                                   | one-writer log bound to the       |\\\\n|                                   | principal at attachment; unproven |\\\\n|                                   | is `:unauthenticated`; retraction |\\\\n|                                   | needs matching proof (8.2). Key   |\\\\n|                                   | management stays open (12).       |\\\\n| Host modules implied a sandbox    | Agreed. The host module is named  |\\\\n|                                   | the trusted composition boundary; |\\\\n|                                   | the profile check is a declaration|\\\\n|                                   | check; run-time confinement is    |\\\\n|                                   | the capability-token discipline,  |\\\\n|                                   | outside the linker (8.3).         |\\\\n| Contract checked after row-local  | Agreed; r2 ordering defect. The   |\\\\n| validation                        | contract check is step 0, before  |\\\\n|                                   | any fetch or validator, and is    |\\\\n|                                   | repeated against the manifest's   |\\\\n|                                   | contract before any image fetch   |\\\\n|                                   | (4.2 step 0, 8.1).                |\\\\n\\\\n### 13.10 Revision r2 (round 1, `gpt-5.6-sol`)\\\\n\\\\nEach finding of the 2026-09-24 adversarial review, and what r2 did.\\\\n\\\\n| Finding                          | Resolution                          |\\\\n|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\\\\n| P1 AST hash drops the tag        | Agreed. `:identity-fn` and          |\\\\n|                                  | `:identity-matches-fn` take the     |\\\\n|                                  | body as fetched; the id is          |\\\\n|                                  | prepended only for step 4 (5.1).    |\\\\n| P1 request carries functions     | Agreed. Closed plain-data request   |\\\\n|                                  | key set; indexes, formats, name     |\\\\n|                                  | environment in `link-state`;        |\\\\n|                                  | `:invalid-request` (6.2, 6.3).      |\\\\n|                                  | Divergence: receiver capabilities   |\\\\n|                                  | are not sent either way; step 5b    |\\\\n|                                  | runs at the receiver against live   |\\\\n|                                  | state (4.2). A remote index is      |\\\\n|                                  | observed into local state, not      |\\\\n|                                  | queried inline (4.2 step 1).        |\\\\n| P1 names, not profiles           | Agreed. Obligations joined from     |\\\\n|                                  | manifest declarations; exact        |\\\\n|                                  | profile / manifest equality;        |\\\\n|                                  | `:undeclared-free` (4.2 step 5).    |\\\\n| P1 wait entry incomplete         | Agreed. `:link-request` and         |\\\\n|                                  | `:link-response`; cursor minted     |\\\\n|                                  | before append; envelope and both    |\\\\n|                                  | descriptors retained (7.2 steps 3-4)|\\\\n| P1 correlation unspecified       | Agreed. Exact id match; per-entry   |\\\\n|                                  | kept cursor advancing past others;  |\\\\n|                                  | duplicate, late, unknown, abandoned |\\\\n|                                  | fixed (7.2 step 7). Divergence:     |\\\\n|                                  | per-entry cells rather than one     |\\\\n|                                  | router cursor, so a parked entry    |\\\\n|                                  | stays a UCF cell and migrates.      |\\\\n| P1 install cannot be synchronous | Agreed; r1's claim withdrawn.       |\\\\n|                                  | Scheduler child task with phases,   |\\\\n|                                  | own store and wait-set, waiter set, |\\\\n|                                  | `:install` wait reason (7.3).       |\\\\n| P1 four-way pairing unverified   | Agreed. `:yin.module/tree` plus one |\\\\n|                                  | `derive-record` per format;         |\\\\n|                                  | input/output checked; verifying or  |\\\\n|                                  | trusted, named; swapped-image tests |\\\\n|                                  | for all formats (8.1, M4).          |\\\\n| P1 manifest name unchecked       | Agreed. `:module-name-mismatch`     |\\\\n|                                  | after manifest validation (8.1).    |\\\\n| P1 authority open at M4          | Agreed. Fail-closed assertion       |\\\\n|                                  | policy with asserter, snapshot,     |\\\\n|                                  | retraction, `:ambiguous-name`; M4   |\\\\n|                                  | entry criterion (8.2, M4).          |\\\\n|                                  | Authentication of principals stays  |\\\\n|                                  | open and is named as such (12).     |\\\\n| P2 decoupling not strict         | Agreed as stated: strict decoupling |\\\\n|                                  | is H/R only, said plainly (3); the  |\\\\n|                                  | pinning amendment is recorded as a  |\\\\n|                                  | UCF / code-as-tuples decision (12). |\\\\n| P2 unary segment-key at step 3   | Agreed. `segment-matches?` for the  |\\\\n|                                  | storage-derived formats (4.1, 5).   |\\\\n| P2 unbounded parts               | Agreed. `:row-defect-fn` before     |\\\\n|                                  | enqueue; `:max-parts`, `:max-depth`,|\\\\n|                                  | `:max-bytes`; `:parts-limit` (4.2). |\\\\n| P2 fetch's handle is a backdoor  | Agreed. `fetch` takes a link        |\\\\n|                                  | runtime with a driver; no handle;   |\\\\n|                                  | traffic-equivalence test (6.4, M3). |\\\\n| P2 DHT is not a stream endpoint  | Agreed. DHT handle behind           |\\\\n|                                  | `serve-content!`; equivalence       |\\\\n|                                  | claimed at the linker boundary only;|\\\\n|                                  | stepped client out of scope (6.1).  |\\\\n| P2 host modules unconstrained    | Agreed. Profile classes enforced at |\\\\n|                                  | registration; `:host` refused;      |\\\\n|                                  | effectful exports return effect     |\\\\n|                                  | data only (8.3).                    |\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n16:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_7\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\nname: code-review-pr\\\\ndescription: >-\\\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\\\n\\\\n## Overview\\\\n\\\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\\\n\\\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Quick Start\\\\n\\\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\\\n\\\\n# 2. Review last commit\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\\\n\\\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\\\n\\\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Code Review Workflow\\\\n\\\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\\\n\\\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\\\n\\\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\\\n\\\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\\\n\\\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\\\n\\\\n```markdown\\\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\\\n\\\\n## Executive Summary\\\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\\\n\\\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\\\n  ```diff\\\\n  - problematic code\\\\n  + suggested fix\\\\n  ```\\\\n\\\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\\\n\\\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\\\n\\\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Utility Script Reference\\\\n\\\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\\\n\\\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\\\n\\\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n58:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_29\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"# M4 S3 commit gate\\\\n\\\\n**Verdict: REJECT.** The orchestrator reports passing JVM, Node, and Dart suites, but the diff does not meet the linker spec’s resource isolation requirements. I did not rerun the suites.\\\\n\\\\n## Findings\\\\n\\\\n- **P0 — Program code can reach engine resources through the task store.** Stream handles and cursor cells are still written into `:store`, and stream effects accept a program supplied reference by its predictable id without a seal check ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:254), [engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:277), [engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:297)). The new test proves only that a *module closure* cannot read the parent store; it confirms the live handle remains there ([linker_require_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:718)). Top level code can read that store, and a literal reference can name an existing resource at effect dispatch. The lift’s refusal of stream and cursor references closes the export path it tests, but does not close these runtime paths. The private resource table and sealed reference checks required by linker r8–r11 are must-fix for this gate.\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — Install publication can throw instead of refusing the install.** `link-install` catches lift failures, but image attachment, decoding, store lowering, and dependency publication occur outside that catch ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:805), [engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:822)). `advance-install` calls `link-install` outside its child-run catch ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:853)). A lowering defect can therefore escape the scheduler round while the install and its waiters remain parked, instead of producing the specified refusal.\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — The register continuation’s image table is insufficiently bound to the code space.** The new validator checks rows individually, but does not require ordered, nonoverlapping, complete coverage of the concatenated instructions; it also accepts any zero-length row without checking its identity ([debruijn_register_effects.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc:233)). The added test checks one forged identity, not missing, overlapping, or reordered rows ([attach_image_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/attach_image_test.cljc:364)). The legitimate parked-after-attach case is fixed, but a supplied table can misdescribe which verified images constitute the payload.\\\\n\\\\n## Gate checks\\\\n\\\\n`require` remains an ordinary effect producing function; the changed handler emits a name, format, and contract request and does not itself resolve a name outside the linker’s authority policy ([module.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:411)). The Rule R registry check and its tests address the reserved `yin/def` path and export keys ([vm.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1867), [rule_r_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/rule_r_test.cljc:425)). The store-write audit allowlist change follows the move of program writes to `engine/put-active` and does not add a broad exception ([store_write_audit_test.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:292)).\\\\n\\\\nThe orchestrator’s ClojureDart fix uses an explicit `:cljd` branch for `runtimeType` ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:476)). I found no second comparably clear ClojureDart unsafe form in the changed paths. The report’s deferred `:foreign-image` test leaves that guard unverified: the guard is reached after lift only when a closure origin is absent from the install image and linked registry images ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:774), [engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:810)); the current tests do not establish its refusal behavior.\\\\n\\\\n**Verdict: REJECT. Must fix:** private resources and sealed reference validation with resource lowering; convert publication and lowering failures into install refusals; validate the register image table’s complete, exact correspondence to the code space.\\\"}}\\n  [resource]\\n  (and (map? resource) (contains? resource :stream-id)))\\n\\n\\n(defn issue-ref\\n  \\\"A reference of `kind` (`:stream-ref` or `:cursor-ref`) to resource\\n   `id`, sealed under the task's capability secret. A task composed\\n   without a secret cannot issue one: it fails closed rather than issue a\\n   reference anyone could forge.\\\"\\n  [state kind id]\\n  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)]\\n    (when (nil? secret)\\n      (fail (str \\\"This task has no capability secret, so it cannot issue\\\"\\n                 \\\" a resource reference\\\")\\n            {:reason :no-capability-secret, :kind kind, :id id}))\\n    {:type kind, :id id, :seal (seal-of secret kind id)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn authentic-ref?\\n  \\\"True when `ref` is a `kind` reference this task issued: its seal\\n   verifies under the task's secret, and it names a live resource of that\\n   kind.\\\"\\n  [state kind ref]\\n  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)\\n        id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))\\n        resource (get (:resources state) id)]\\n    (boolean (and (some? secret)\\n                  (= kind (:type ref))\\n                  (some? resource)\\n                  (= (:seal ref) (seal-of secret kind id))\\n                  (= (= :cursor-ref kind) (cursor-cell? resource))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn check-ref!\\n  \\\"The resource id a program-supplied `kind` reference names, once its\\n   seal verifies (r10). Anything else fails closed with\\n   `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it named.\\\"\\n  [state effect-kind kind ref]\\n  (if (authentic-ref? state kind ref)\\n    (:id ref)\\n    (fail \\\"Forged resource reference\\\"\\n          {:reason :forged-resource-reference,\\n           :effect effect-kind,\\n           :kind kind,\\n           :id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))})))\\n\\n\\n(declare gensym)\\n\\n\\n(defn attach-resource\\n  \\\"Install the host stream `handle` a composition hands a task under a\\n   fresh resource id, and return `[stream-ref state]` with the reference\\n   sealed under the task's secret. This is how a stream reaches a program:\\n   never through the store.\\\"\\n  [state handle]\\n  (let [[id state] (gensym state \\\"stream\\\")\\n        ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)]\\n    [ref (assoc-in state [:resources id] handle)]))\\n\\n\\n(defn make-woken-run-queue-entries\\n  \\\"Transform woken wait-set entries into ready-queue entries.\\n   Readers (with :cursor-ref) record the successor cursor the transport\\n   returned as a resource update. Writers (no :cursor-ref) just stamp\\n   :value. Each woken result's :status rides onto the ready entry, where\\n   terminal-resume-outcome reads it.\\n\\n   The ready entry stays pure data: any `:stream` handle a poll resolved\\n   is dropped -- the resource updates carry the successor cursor, and\\n   handles are re-resolved from ids when needed again -- so an entry\\n   neither waits nor runs holding a host object.\\\"\\n  [state woken]\\n  (mapv (fn [{:keys [entry value cursor status], :as woken-entry}]\\n          (let [cursor-ref (:cursor-ref entry)\\n                updates\\n                (or (:resource-updates woken-entry)\\n                    (when (and cursor-ref cursor)\\n                      (let [cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\\n                            cursor-data (get (:resources state) cursor-id)]\\n                        {cursor-id (assoc cursor-data :cursor cursor)})))]\\n            (dissoc (assoc entry\\n                           :value value\\n                           :resource-updates updates\\n                           :status status\\n                           :cursor cursor)\\n                    :stream)))\\n        woken))\\n\\n\\n(defn handle-make\\n  \\\"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\\n   `:make-stream`, holds the handle in the private `:resources` table, and\\n   answers a sealed reference; the reference is issued first, so a task\\n   with no secret creates no stream.\\n\\n   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\\n   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\\n   silently falling back to a private transport is the failure mode the rule\\n   exists to prevent.\\n   Returns [stream-ref updated-state].\\\"\\n  [state effect id]\\n  (let [capacity (or (:capacity effect) vm/default-stream-capacity)\\n        stream-ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)\\n        handle (vm/create-stream! (:make-stream state) capacity :stream/make)]\\n    [stream-ref (assoc-in state [:resources id] handle)]))\\n\\n\\n(defn handle-put\\n  \\\"Handle :stream/put. Total over the five append outcomes, once the\\n   reference verifies.\\n   Returns {:value v :state s} on success, {:park true :stream-id id :state s}\\n   on `full`. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are errors that\\n   name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\\\"\\n  [state effect]\\n  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/put :stream-ref (:stream effect))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:260:                                          :capability-secret :secret-source\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:269:            :capability-secret (:capability-secret base),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:270:            :secret-source (:secret-source base),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:760:    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:772:                :capability-secret capability-secret,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:773:                :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:245:                                          :capability-secret :secret-source\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:254:            :capability-secret (:capability-secret base),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:255:            :secret-source (:secret-source base),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:778:    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:790:                :capability-secret capability-secret,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:791:                :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:254:(defn issue-ref\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:260:  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:264:            {:reason :no-capability-secret, :kind kind, :id id}))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:268:(defn authentic-ref?\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:273:  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:283:(defn check-ref!\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:288:  (if (authentic-ref? state kind ref)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:300:(defn attach-resource\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:307:        ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:353:        stream-ref (issue-ref state :stream-ref id)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:365:  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/put :stream-ref (:stream effect))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:384:  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/cursor :stream-ref (:stream effect))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:385:        cursor-ref (issue-ref state :cursor-ref id)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:402:        cursor-id (check-ref! state :stream/next :cursor-ref cursor-ref)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:434:  (let [stream-id (check-ref! state :stream/close :stream-ref (:stream effect))]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:563:                  (not (authentic-ref? vm (:type x) x))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:617:                (issue-ref vm :stream-ref\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:620:                (issue-ref vm :cursor-ref (get cells (:yin.k/cell x)))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:917:   `:secret-source`, a function of the child's origin tag; a composition\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:927:        source (:secret-source state)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:937:                                    :capability-secret\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:891:                                     :capability-secret :secret-source\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:935:    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:948:                    :capability-secret capability-secret,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:949:                    :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:76:   capability-secret ; this task's secret; every reference is sealed by it\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:77:   secret-source  ; the composition's minting of child task secrets\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:128:                   (:capability-secret vm)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:129:                   (:secret-source vm)\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:1051:                                     :capability-secret :secret-source\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:1090:    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:1104:                     :capability-secret capability-secret,\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:1105:                     :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1932:     :capability-secret this task's capability secret (yin.vm.linker.md\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1938:     :secret-source (fn [origin] -> secret): the composition's minting of\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2041:        :capability-secret (:capability-secret opts),\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2042:        :secret-source (:secret-source opts),\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n23:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'secret-source|close|closed|capability secret' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | head -55; sed -n '1025,1070p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc; sed -n '180,220p' src/clj/yin/demo.clj; sed -n '140,195p' src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc; rg -n 'create-vm|make-vm|secret-source' src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"114:9. A fail-closed, proof-carrying name-authority policy for `dao.space`\\n181:**I8. Fail closed.** Every ambiguity the linker can detect (two manifests\\n294:  lambda body encloses the definition; `:in-body?` is true when a\\n295:  lambda body encloses the occurrence; an `:applications-fn` record is\\n364:   the request's shape against the closed key set of section 6.3\\n443:       jump target, or any lambda body encloses it. A conditional\\n516:     closed-image case and remains supported.\\n546:| `:invalid-request`      | 0      | a request outside the closed key set of  |\\n832:A `request` is plain data and nothing else. Its closed key set is\\n844:;; by bare identity: B6's closed-image path, no manifest\\n1353:  elsewhere fails closed rather than being reinterpreted. Converting a\\n1483:  are bound to a **task-scoped capability secret**, minted once by\\n1491:  with a wrong or missing seal fails closed with\\n1494:  last read path, and r10 closes it. Lift and lower re-seal rather\\n1504:  own capability secret; an invalid, unsealed, or forged reference\\n1693:**Fail-closed assertion policy.** This policy is an entry criterion for\\n2304:The round-10 review closed the citation and file-box item and left\\n2330:The round-9 review closed the r8 resource-lowering item and raised\\n2348:|                                   | missing seal fails closed with    |\\n2369:The round-8 review closed the walker-provenance item (no action);\\n2399:The round-7 review closed the grow-only-returns item (no action)\\n2438:The round-6 review closed the dominance and scanner item (no\\n2486:The round-5 review closed the wait-entry id and duplicate-authority\\n2535:The round-4 review closed round-3 item 5 and left four items partial.\\n2574:The round-3 review closed round-2 items 1, 3, 5, 7, and 10 and left four\\n2621:closed and marked seven items partial plus one ordering defect. Each,\\n2723:| P1 authority open at M4          | Agreed. Fail-closed assertion       |\\n     :call-out      explicit outbound response handle\\n     :call-capacity capacity for a constructed FFI pair\\n     :bridge        host FFI handlers\\n\\n   There is no `:in-stream`: program observation belongs to\\n   `dao.stream.observer`, and an obsolete `:in-stream` option is\\n   rejected here, before any FFI resource is allocated.\\n\\n   Construction is all-or-nothing: creating the FFI pair and minting the\\n   call-out cursor are stream operations, and any non-`ok` outcome fails here\\n   rather than leaving a half-built VM. The bridge cursor is minted by\\n   `ffi/attach` below; the program cursor belongs to observer attachment.\\\"\\n  ([] (create-vm {}))\\n  ([opts]\\n   (when (contains? opts :in-stream)\\n     (throw (ex-info\\n              \\\"Program observation moved to dao.stream.observer: a VM no longer accepts :in-stream\\\"\\n              {:in-stream (:in-stream opts)})))\\n   (let [env (vm/check-bindings! :env (or (:env opts) {}))\\n         base (vm/empty-state\\n                (assoc (select-keys opts\\n                                    [:primitives :primitive-profiles\\n                                     :primitive-canonical-names :modules\\n                                     :make-stream :call-in :call-out\\n                                     :call-capacity :link-request\\n                                     :link-response :origin :ancestry\\n                                     :capability-secret :secret-source\\n                                     :attach-stream])\\n                       :telemetry (:telemetry opts)\\n                       :vm-model :ast-walker))]\\n     (-> (map->ASTWalkerVM (merge base\\n                                  {:bridge nil,\\n                                   :program nil,\\n                                   :control nil,\\n                                   :env env,\\n                                   :k nil,\\n                                   :value nil,\\n                                   :halted? true,\\n                                   :blocked? false}))\\n         (telemetry/install :ast-walker)\\n         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))\\n         (telemetry/emit-snapshot :init)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Module kernel (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3)\\n  (println \\\"Computing sum(0..100) = 5050\\\")\\n  (println\\n    \\\"Each VM adds one number, then passes its continuation to the other.\\\")\\n  (println \\\"Continuations travel as batches through a DaoStream v2 medium.\\\")\\n  (println)\\n  ;; One shared medium. The composition is its only writer and, across the\\n  ;; two VMs taking turns, its only reader.\\n  (let [medium (new-stream)\\n        writer medium\\n        reader-cursor* (atom (vm/mint-oldest medium :continuation-reader))\\n        ;; Create two VMs and define 'step on both\\n        vm-a (-> (ast-walker/create-vm {:env {}, :primitives vm/primitives,\\n                                        :make-stream make-stream,\\n                                        :capability-secret\\n                                        (str (random-uuid))})\\n                 (vm/eval define-step-ast))\\n        vm-b (-> (ast-walker/create-vm {:env {}, :primitives vm/primitives,\\n                                        :make-stream make-stream,\\n                                        :capability-secret\\n                                        (str (random-uuid))})\\n                 (vm/eval define-step-ast))]\\n    (println \\\"VM-A and VM-B initialized. 'step' function defined on both.\\\")\\n    (println)\\n    ;; VM-A starts the computation: (step 0 0)\\n    ;; This will compute 0+0=0, then park.\\n    (loop [current-vm (vm/eval vm-a call-step-ast)\\n           other-vm vm-b\\n           current-name \\\"VM-A\\\"\\n           other-name \\\"VM-B\\\"\\n           cursor @reader-cursor*\\n           step-count 0]\\n      (cond\\n        ;; Computation complete: VM halted without parking\\n        (and (vm/halted? current-vm) (empty? (:parked current-vm)))\\n        (do (println)\\n            (println (format \\\"=== %s computed final result: %d ===\\\"\\n                             current-name\\n                             (vm/value current-vm)))\\n            (println (format \\\"Total continuation transfers: %d\\\" step-count))\\n            (reset! reader-cursor* cursor)\\n            (vm/value current-vm))\\n  [vm-state]\\n  (into []\\n        (remove composition-key?)\\n        (keys (:resources vm-state))))\\n\\n\\n(defn shippable?\\n  \\\"True when a semantic VM is between instructions with nothing outside its\\n   registers: no wait set, ready queue, or parked continuation, and no stream\\n   handle or cursor cell in its resources.\\\"\\n  [vm-state]\\n  (boolean (and vm-state\\n                (:control vm-state)\\n                (not (:halted? vm-state))\\n                (not (:blocked? vm-state))\\n                (empty? (:wait-set vm-state))\\n                (empty? (:ready-queue vm-state))\\n                (empty? (:parked vm-state))\\n                (empty? (resource-keys vm-state)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn semantic-registers\\n  \\\"The plain-data registers of a shippable semantic VM, stamped with the\\n   semantic contract its segment was admitted under. A primitive the\\n   program resolved (a `+` pushed before its call) travels by name; any other\\n   host function throws, as does a VM holding stream handles, which would\\n   arrive as references to nothing.\\\"\\n  [vm-state]\\n  (when-let [ks (seq (resource-keys vm-state))]\\n    (throw (ex-info \\\"Cannot ship a continuation holding live stream resources\\\"\\n                    {:resource-keys (vec ks)})))\\n  (encode-primitives\\n    (:primitives vm-state)\\n    {:contract vm/semantic-contract,\\n     :segment (get-in vm-state [:control :segment]),\\n     :pc (get-in vm-state [:control :pc]),\\n     :value (:value vm-state),\\n     :stack (:stack vm-state),\\n     :env (:env vm-state),\\n     :k (:k vm-state),\\n     :id-counter (:id-counter vm-state),\\n     :defs (into {} (filter (comp symbol? key)) (:store vm-state))}))\\n\\n\\n(defn continuation-datoms\\n  \\\"The handoff batch for a shippable semantic VM running `code-datoms`: the\\n   segment, then one registers datom carrying EDN text.\\\"\\n  [code-datoms vm-state]\\n  (let [registers (semantic-registers vm-state)\\n        k-eid (dec (reduce min 0 (map first code-datoms)))]\\n    (conj (vec code-datoms)\\n          [k-eid registers-attr (pr-str registers) 0 0])))\\n\\n\\n(defn datoms->semantic-vm\\n  \\\"Rebuild a semantic VM from a received handoff batch: load the shipped\\n15:     `dao.stream.observer` does — and `create-vm` rejects `:in-stream`.\\n56:  [vm-key payload make-vm]\\n57:  (let [base (make-vm vm-key)]\\n196:   segment through the ordinary loader into `(make-vm)`, then install the\\n198:  [batch make-vm]\\n203:        base (make-vm)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n24:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"## Re-gate: M4 S3\\n\\n**P1 — Standard compositions leave install children unable to use streams.** `spawn-child` gives a child a capability secret only when the parent has `:secret-source` ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:927)). The REPL supplies a root `:capability-secret` but no source ([repl.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:426)); `dao.await/run` does the same ([await.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:229)). A module installed through either composition cannot issue a stream reference: `issue-ref` refuses a child without a secret ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:254)). The test covers a deliberately omitted source, but not a stream using module in these compositions ([linker_require_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:888)). Supply fresh child secrets from each composition’s random source.\\n\\nThe **three previous rejection findings are closed** in the reviewed diff. Stream and cursor resources now live in `:resources`, and dispatch and lift authenticate references ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:268), [linker_require_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:752)). Publication exceptions now refuse the install without publishing partial state, with tests for attachment and lowering failures ([engine.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:1043), [linker_require_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:1161)). Register image tables now require exact coverage, with missing, overlapping, reordered, and empty row tests ([debruijn_register_effects.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc:231), [attach_image_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-s3/test/yin/vm/attach_image_test.cljc:396)).\\n\\nI found no further program path from `:store` to the private resources, no seal laundering path in lift and lower, no materially weakened existing assertion, and no clear ClojureDart unsafe form in the changed paths. I did not rerun suites; the orchestrator reports JVM, Node, and Dart passing.\\n\\n**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-FIXES. Must fix:** mint child capability secrets in the REPL and `dao.await` compositions, and test a stream using module through those paths.\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
